      "id": "ak3biyf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Nature of the Revolt"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Revolt of 1857 began at Meerut on May 10, 1857, marking a major turning point in Indian national history.",
        "Economic exploitation, political annexation policies like Doctrine of Lapse, and social interventions were major underlying causes.",
        "The immediate spark was the introduction of greased cartridges for the Enfield rifle.",
        "Key centers and leaders included Rani Lakshmibai (Jhansi), Nana Sahib (Kanpur), Begum Hazrat Mahal (Lucknow), and Kunwar Singh (Bihar).",
        "The Revolt resulted in the passage of the Government of India Act 1858, transferring power from the East India Company directly to the British Crown."
      ],
      "id": "qqkk6im"
    },
